# Target C-II — the small-leaf O--O family has quadratic crossing incidence with the center phase

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only phase-resolved structural theorem  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_small_leaf_phase_outer_pair_weight_t1844_2026-09-15.md`; dominant-orbit triangle-free center/leaf correlation  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The phase-resolved outer-pair theorem gives a retained O--O family of weight `>0.0848D` in the smallest leaf phase of the dominant exact `pi/3` orbit.  This note proves that the family is not an irrelevant low-degree tail: it participates in a quadratic number of actual strict-rhombus crossings with the center phase.

Let the dominant orbit phase masses be

\[
C\ge U\ge V,
\qquad
W=C+U+V,
\]

and let `Q_V` be the retained weighted O--O subfamily in the `V` phase.  Write

\[
P_V=|Q_V|/D
\]

for its normalized retained pair weight.  Then every survivor with

\[
\boxed{t=(q-k)/D\le1.844}
\]

satisfies

\[
\boxed{
E(C,Q_V)>0.072\,D^2-o(D^2),}
\tag{CX.1}

where `E(C,Q_V)` is the weighted number of actual strict-rhombus crossings between center-phase trains and the retained `V`-phase O--O trains.

Consequently at least one `V`-phase O--O train of positive retained weight is crossed by

\[
\boxed{>0.135\,D-o(D)}
\tag{CX.2}

center-phase complete trains.

This produces one concrete outer-to-outer train cut which is simultaneously:

- in an exact leaf phase;
- part of a pairwise-noncrossing linear O--O family;
- crossed by linearly many center-phase trains; and
- two-sidedly braced by actual diameter edges by Section 157.

---

## 1. Safe rounded inputs from the phase-resolved theorem

The exact companion certificate for the parent theorem gives

\[
W<1.915963+o(1),
\tag{CX.3}

\[
E_{dom}>0.9068958-o(1),
\tag{CX.4}

\[
C>0.8538-o(1),
\tag{CX.5}

and retained outer occurrence mass in the smallest leaf phase

\[
z_V>0.616-o(1).
\tag{CX.6}

For every `V`-phase train `tau`, let `w_tau=min(a_tau,b_tau)` be its retained O--O pair weight.  Summing

\[
a_\tau+b_\tau\le1+w_\tau
\]

gives

\[
P_V\ge z_V-V-o(1).
\tag{CX.7}

Thus, before using the stronger numerical `0.0848` conclusion, retain the variable lower bound

\[
\boxed{P_V\ge0.616-V-o(1).}
\tag{CX.8}

This dependence on `V` is needed in the crossing optimization.

---

## 2. Missing center--leaf crossings are controlled by the orbit defect

Let

\[
x=CU-e(C,U),
\qquad
y=CV-e(C,V)
\]

be the missing crossing masses in the two center/leaf pairs.  Put

\[
h:=C(U+V)-E_{dom}.
\tag{CX.9}

The hostile-audited triangle-free center-degree argument gives

\[
\left(1-{V\over C}\right)x
+
\left(1-{U\over C}\right)y
\le h.
\tag{CX.10}

Set

\[
b:=1-{U\over C}\ge0.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{y\le CV,}
\tag{CX.11}

and whenever `b>0`,

\[
\boxed{y\le {h\over b}.}
\tag{CX.12}

The weighted O--O subfamily `Q_V` is a submeasure of the full `V`-phase train measure.  The total missing center incidences involving that submeasure are therefore at most the full missing mass `y`.  Hence

\[
E(C,Q_V)
\ge C P_V-y-o(1).
\]

Using (CX.8),

\[
\boxed{
{E(C,Q_V)\over D^2}
\ge
C(0.616-V)-\min\left\{CV,{h\over b}\right\}-o(1).}
\tag{CX.13}

It remains to minimize this expression under the safe rounded global constraints.

---

## 3. Push the adverse total mass and crossing defect to their safe endpoints

Use

\[
W_0=1.915963,
\qquad
E_0=0.9068958.
\tag{CX.14}

For fixed `C,V`, write

\[
U=W-C-V,
\qquad
b={2C+V-W\over C},
\]

and

\[
h=C(W-C)-E_{dom}.
\]

Whenever `b>0`, the quantity

\[
{h\over b}
=
{C[C(W-C)-E_{dom}]\over2C+V-W}
\]

increases with `W` and decreases with `E_dom`.  Therefore the adverse choice is the largest `W` permitted by both (CX.3) and the ordering `U<=C`:

\[
W_*:=\min\{W_0,2C+V\},
\]

and the smallest crossing mass `E_0`.

This reduces the proof to two finite branches.

---

## 4. Ordering-limited branch: `2C+V<=W_0`

Here the adverse total mass reaches the ordering boundary `U=C`, so `b=0` and only the trivial missing bound `y<=CV` is used.

Equation (CX.13) gives

\[
{E(C,Q_V)\over D^2}
\ge C(0.616-2V)-o(1).
\]

The branch condition gives

\[
V\le W_0-2C
\le1.915963-2(0.8538)
=0.208363.
\]

Therefore

\[
C(0.616-2V)
>0.1701>0.072.
\tag{CX.15}

This branch is far from controlling.

---

## 5. Full-mass branch: `2C+V>W_0`

Now set `W=W_0`.  Put

\[
H(C):=C(W_0-C)-E_0.
\]

The actual configuration has `H(C)>=0`; otherwise even the generic center capacity would be below the required dominant crossing mass.

For fixed `C`, let

\[
V_{max}={W_0-C\over2}
\]

from the ordering `U>=V`.

The lower bound (CX.13) is

\[
J(V)
=
C(0.616-V)
-
\min\left\{
CV,
{H(C)C\over2C+V-W_0}
\right\}.
\tag{CX.16}

The switch between the two missing-mass bounds is determined by

\[
V(2C+V-W_0)=H(C).
\tag{CX.17}

Before the switch, `J(V)=C(0.616-2V)` decreases with `V`.  After the switch,

\[
J(V)
=C(0.616-V)
-{H(C)C\over2C+V-W_0},
\]

whose second derivative is negative.  Hence on the post-switch interval it is concave and attains its minimum at an endpoint.

Therefore the minimum over all `V` occurs either

1. at the switch (CX.17), or
2. at the balanced-leaf endpoint `V=V_max`.

### Switch point

Globally,

\[
H(C)\le {W_0^2\over4}-E_0
<0.010833.
\]

If a switch occurred with `V>=0.255`, then using `C>0.8538`,

\[
V(2C+V-W_0)
>0.255[2(0.8538)+0.255-1.915963]
>0.01189,
\]

contradicting the preceding `H` ceiling.  Thus every switch has

\[
V<0.255.
\]

At the switch,

\[
J=C(0.616-2V)
>0.8538(0.616-0.510)
>0.0905>0.072.
\tag{CX.18}

### Balanced-leaf endpoint

Set

\[
V=U={W_0-C\over2}.
\]

Then

\[
b={3C-W_0\over2C}>0
\]

and the nontrivial missing bound gives

\[
J_{bal}(C)
= C\left(0.616-{W_0-C\over2}\right)
-
{2C[C(W_0-C)-E_0]\over3C-W_0}.
\tag{CX.19}

The denominator is positive for `C>=0.8538`.  To prove `J_bal(C)>0.072`, multiply by `3C-W_0` and define

\[
N(C):=[J_{bal}(C)-0.072](3C-W_0).
\]

With the exact rational rounded constants in (CX.14), `N` is the cubic

\[
\begin{aligned}
N(C)=\frac12[&-6(0.072)C+2(0.072)W_0
+6(0.616)C^2-2(0.616)CW_0\\
&+7C^3-8C^2W_0+4CE_0+CW_0^2].
\end{aligned}
\tag{CX.20}

The exact verifier proves:

- `N(0.8538)>0`;
- `N''(C)>0` for every `C>=0.8538`;
- `N'(0.8575)<0<N'(0.8576)`; and
- on the only critical interval `[0.8575,0.8576]`, an exact derivative-Lipschitz bound gives
  \[
  N(C)>0.00031564.
  \]

Thus

\[
\boxed{J_{bal}(C)>0.072}
\tag{CX.21}

for every feasible `C`.

Combining (CX.15), (CX.18), and (CX.21) proves (CX.1).

---

## 6. One leaf O--O train crosses linearly many center trains

The total retained O--O pair weight in the `V` phase is at most the total `V`-phase train mass.  The parent theorem gives

\[
P_V\le V<0.5311+o(1).
\]

If every positive-weight `V`-phase O--O train were crossed by at most `dD` center trains, then the weighted crossing incidence would be at most

\[
dD\cdot P_VD
<0.5311dD^2+o(D^2).
\]

Equation (CX.1) therefore forces one train with

\[
d>{0.072\over0.5311}>0.1355.
\]

Hence at least one retained `V`-phase O--O train is crossed by more than

\[
0.135D-o(D)
\]

center-phase complete trains, proving (CX.2).

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The live band

\[
1.843555\lesssim t\le1.844
\]

now contains a specific train object with four simultaneous properties:

```text
one exact leaf phase;
outer-to-outer;
Section-157 two-sided actual-diameter bracing;
>0.135 D strict-rhombus crossings with the exact center phase.
```

Moreover the entire leaf-phase O--O family is pairwise noncrossing and carries `>0.072D^2` weighted center-crossing incidence.

This is the first current structural reduction that places a macroscopic exact-phase crossing fan on a single braced outer-to-outer train.  The next step should use the order of the `>0.135D` center crossings along that train together with the nested order of same-phase O--O cuts.  Taking differences of the Section-157 KKT cut resultants between adjacent leaf cuts is a natural route: it localizes the diameter resultant to the strip between neighboring parallel trains and may prevent the same two diameter edges from bracing a linear number of cuts.

No Level-I closure is claimed.  C-II remains open.
